import{_ as o,q as t,o as l,c as d,b as e,k as c,j as i,m as n,y as h}from"./chunks/framework.D3-xQjZb.js";const y=JSON.parse('{"title":"What Should Happen When You Click an AI Citation?","description":"A proposed briefing interface connects each claim to its original passage, keeps competing accounts visible, and explains why an assessment changed.","frontmatter":{"title":"What Should Happen When You Click an AI Citation?","description":"A proposed briefing interface connects each claim to its original passage, keeps competing accounts visible, and explains why an assessment changed.","teaser":"A citation can open a 200-page document and still leave you guessing. A useful briefing should take you to the passage, the interpretation, and the strongest competing account.","articleGenre":"Interface design proposal"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"research/systems/vision-and-interface.md","filePath":"research/systems/vision-and-interface.md"}'),p={name:"research/systems/vision-and-interface.md"};function u(m,a,g,f,w,b){const r=t("ArticleHeader"),s=t("ReaderTable");return l(),d("div",null,[a[1]||(a[1]=e("h1",{id:"from-claims-to-navigable-knowledge",tabindex:"-1"},[c("What Should Happen When You Click an AI Citation? "),e("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#from-claims-to-navigable-knowledge","aria-label":'Permalink to "What Should Happen When You Click an AI Citation? {#from-claims-to-navigable-knowledge}"'},"​")],-1)),i(r),a[2]||(a[2]=n('<p>A briefing says, “Two independent reports describe a light near the ridge.” If you were the editor, what would you want a click on <em>independent</em> to show? Here is a design sketch with two invented reports.</p><ol><li><strong>Select the claim.</strong> The proposed briefing opens a panel with the sentence and its two supporting passages, from a witness interview in Report A and a later account in Report B.</li><li><strong>Inspect the quotation.</strong> In A the witness says, “I saw a light near the ridge.” B reproduces the words and credits the interview in A. Opening the passage in context reveals who is speaking in each document.</li><li><strong>Flag the duplicate.</strong> Mark B as quoting A and send the independence claim for review. The editor can keep both documents and revise the briefing to say that both draw on one witness.</li></ol><p>In a usability study I would time how long a reader takes to reach the quoted passage and score whether they identify A as the parent and flag the independence claim correctly. The walkthrough describes the interface I want to test.</p><h2 id="the-central-design-decision-claims-come-first" tabindex="-1">The central design decision: claims come first <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-central-design-decision-claims-come-first" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The central design decision: claims come first&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The unit I would store is an attributed claim. It names who said what, connects that statement to an original passage or timecode, and records the grounds for the current assessment. A government archive can establish who published a report. Whether the report supports the briefing’s wording is a separate question.</p><p>For each consequential sentence I want three answers. What do we know from the available sources? Why does that evidence support these particular words? What new information could change the assessment?</p><h2 id="two-different-graphs" tabindex="-1">Two different graphs <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-different-graphs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two different graphs&quot;">​</a></h2>',7)),i(s,{label:"Table: Two different graphs"},{default:h(()=>[...a[0]||(a[0]=[e("table",null,[e("thead",null,[e("tr",null,[e("th",null,"When you are reading"),e("th",null,"When you are checking")])]),e("tbody",null,[e("tr",null,[e("td",null,"A link leads from an event page to a person, place, or related topic."),e("td",null,"An evidence link identifies what a source said about that person or event.")]),e("tr",null,[e("td",null,"A useful measure is how quickly someone finds the relevant topic."),e("td",null,"A useful measure is whether someone can locate the exact supporting or conflicting passage.")])])],-1)])]),_:1}),a[3]||(a[3]=n('<p>The navigation graph organizes pages. The evidence graph records sources, claims, entities, and the relationships among them. One helps you find a name. The other helps you decide whether it belongs in the sentence.</p><h2 id="wikipedia-and-wikidata-are-external-sources-not-ui-templates" tabindex="-1">Wikipedia and Wikidata are external sources, not UI templates <a class="header-anchor" href="#wikipedia-and-wikidata-are-external-sources-not-ui-templates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Wikipedia and Wikidata are external sources, not UI templates&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A Wikipedia link and its surrounding text can suggest a candidate topic. Wikidata offers identifiers, aliases, qualifiers, and references. Preserve a stable local identifier, the external source and revision, and the evidence used to resolve the match. If the match changes later, a reader should still be able to reconstruct the earlier interpretation. <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Data_model" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Wikidata’s data model</a> · <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Help:Data_access" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Accessing Wikidata</a></p><h2 id="the-intended-interface" tabindex="-1">The intended interface <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-intended-interface" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The intended interface&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The briefing opens with what changed and why it matters. Select a claim to open a short, versioned dossier with the chronology, supporting passages, and competing accounts. Exact search should find a name or document number. Semantic search should find related descriptions. A focused graph view can show how a small group of sources depends on one another.</p><p>A contextual assistant should follow the selected passage. Ask “Why did the assessment change?” and it should show the new record, the old assessment, and the decision that connects them. A Five Whys investigation can help trace the system’s reasoning. When the evidence ends, the trace should say so.</p><h2 id="how-ordo-informs-this-interface" tabindex="-1">How Ordo informs this interface <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-ordo-informs-this-interface" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How Ordo informs this interface&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Ordo design documents describe daily briefings, stored artifacts, contextual conversation, and explicit approvals. A typed representation could let a web client and a native Swift client present the same versioned material. The documents establish the design. A released native Swift graph application would be a separate engineering milestone. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/ordo/blob/main/development-docs/PRODUCT-UX.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Product and UX model</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/ordo/blob/main/agentic-appliance/architecture/briefing-ui-runtime.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Briefing runtime</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/ordo/blob/main/agentic-appliance/patterns/artifact-spec-renderer.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Artifact renderer</a></p><h2 id="briefing-as-a-quality-standard-not-military-styling" tabindex="-1">Briefing as a quality standard, not military styling <a class="header-anchor" href="#briefing-as-a-quality-standard-not-military-styling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Briefing as a quality standard, not military styling&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A briefing earns its short form by making qualifications easy to inspect. The intelligence community’s published analytic standards offer useful criteria: assess the source, distinguish information from judgment, consider alternatives, and explain changes. A transcription confidence score and a reviewer’s authority to publish answer different questions. Give each a clear label. <a href="https://www.intelligence.gov/mission/our-values/objectivity" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ICD 203 analytic standards</a></p><h2 id="what-needs-empirical-evaluation" tabindex="-1">What needs empirical evaluation <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-needs-empirical-evaluation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What needs empirical evaluation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Give participants the same documents in three interfaces: a document list, an ordinary chat, and a briefing with optional evidence views. Ask them to spot a copied claim, correct an incorrect source link, and explain a changed assessment. Measure accuracy, time, and cognitive load. A graph deserves the space it takes only if readers complete these tasks more effectively with it.</p><p><span id="related-2026-research"></span></p><h2 id="research-on-claims-and-entity-linking" tabindex="-1">Research on claims and entity linking <a class="header-anchor" href="#research-on-claims-and-entity-linking" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Research on claims and entity linking&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Vitali and Pasqual’s preprint treats statements as attributable claims with potentially competing interpretations. Kakatkar and Khatib’s working paper examines how to represent and explain the status of claims. A systematic review of Wikidata entity linking addresses disambiguation, including the complications of time and qualified relationships. These are starting points for a comparison. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15246" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Provenance-Enhanced Statements</a> · <a href="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7525242" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Epistemic Knowledge Graphs</a> · <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3795134" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Entity Linking with Wikidata</a></p><p><a href="./">Research hub</a> · <a href="./uap-research-testbed">Why UAP is the testbed</a> · <a href="./dissertation-proposal">Advisor proposal</a></p>',16))])}const v=o(p,[["render",u]]);export{y as __pageData,v as default};
